    "result_text": "The build passes. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change was localized to one file, because membership number (and all other derived values) are built by concatenating the `customerCode` at runtime rather than storing a copy of the old format.\n\n**`AssignCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 changed the format from `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (global sequence) to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`:\n- **CITY3** \u2014 upper-cased first three letters of the owner's city\n- **LAST3** \u2014 upper-cased first three letters of the last name\n- **NNNN** \u2014 a *per-city* 4-digit zero-padded sequence: one more than the number of owners already in that city (counted via `findAll()` filtered by normalized city, matching the existing `AssignNamesakeCount`/`AssignHousehold` pattern, since the repository has no count-by-city method).\n\n**No change needed to `AssignMembershipNumber`** \u2014 it produces `<customerCode>-M<YY>` by reading `getCustomerCode()`, so it automatically inherits the new city-prefixed format (e.g. `LON-SMI-0001-M26`). That satisfies the \"every value derived from the customerCode must be rebuilt on the new format\" requirement.\n\nTest result: `AcceptanceTest.coreCustomerCodeHasCityPrefix` (asserting `[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}`) passes, and the full `./mvnw test` run reports **BUILD SUCCESS** with 0 failures/errors.",
